{"profile":{"name":"Alejandra Bravo","ward":"Ward 9, Davenport","bio":"Alejandra Bravo was first elected to Toronto City Council on October 24, 2022, taking office November 15, 2022 with over 70 percent of the vote in the open-seat race to succeed Ana Bailão. She previously ran for council in Davenport in 2003, 2006 and 2014, and was the federal NDP candidate in Davenport in 2021. Born in Chile in 1971, she moved to Canada with her family as a refugee as a child; she was Director of Leadership and Training at the Broadbent Institute and spent a decade as Manager of Leadership and Learning at the Maytree Foundation.","committees":["Economic and Community Development Committee (Chair)","Board of Health","Francophone Affairs Advisory Committee (Chair)","Executive Committee","Toronto and East York Community Council","Toronto Arts Council","Long-Term Care Committee of Management (Chair)"],"contact":{"email":"councillor_bravo@toronto.ca","phone":"416-392-7012"},"promises":[{"text":"End exclusionary zoning, waive City permits and fees to support new affordable housing, and create a dedicated planning team to expedite permits and approvals.","source":"https://storeys.com/toronto-new-city-councillor-elects/"},{"text":"Ensure City-owned land is used to build new mixed-income affordable housing communities.","source":"https://storeys.com/toronto-new-city-councillor-elects/"},{"text":"Fight for stronger protections from renovictions and unfair rent increases, and advocate for a local bylaw setting a maximum allowable temperature in all apartments.","source":"https://storeys.com/toronto-new-city-councillor-elects/"}]},"lobbying":{"total":268,"term":268,"first":"2022-11-17","last":"2026-10-08","lobbyists":71,"clients":59,"subjects":114,"methods":{"Meetings":84,"Emails":159,"Phone calls":15,"Written":7,"Other or unreported":3},"top":[{"label":"Affordable Housing","count":58},{"label":"Economic Development","count":53},{"label":"Planning and Development","count":45},{"label":"By-la